# NerfHack sample .nerfhackrc (Unix/Linux)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is a fully worked example config file, designed to maximize your enjoyment
# of NerfHack's unique features.
#
# Copy it to $HOME/.nerfhackrc and edit freely;
# NerfHack will pick it up automatically. You can also point at a
# different file with the NERFHACKOPTIONS environment variable, e.g.:
#   export NERFHACKOPTIONS="$HOME/.config/nerfhackrc"
#
# Syntax: a '#' starts a comment; everything after it on the line is ignored.
# Blank lines are ignored too. Every setting below uses one of six directive
# keywords (OPTIONS=, AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION=, MENUCOLOR=, MSGTYPE=, BIND=,
# SYMBOLS=) -- each is introduced with a syntax explanation the first time it
# appears, in its own section below.
#
# To disable any line without deleting it, prefix it with '#'. Several
# examples below are intentionally left commented-out to show alternatives
# you can switch to.
#
## Further reading:
# https://nethackwiki.com/wiki/Options#Options
# https://nethack.org/v366/Guidebook.html#toc_9
# In-game: the `opthelp` file installed alongside the binary lists every
# option with a one-line description; `#options` in-game shows current values.
#
## Naming tricks (do these in-game with the #name command, not in this file):
# Naming an item "junk" or "empty" colors it gray and excludes it from
#   autopickup (see the AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION section below for the same
#   effect done automatically by item type).
# Naming the Amulet of Yendor (the real one or a fake) anything at all will
#   highlight it and force autopickup, regardless of the exceptions below.
# Naming a wand that "vanishes" on a test-zap "vanish" or "van" will color it
#   orange (matching the "possibly cancellation" MENUCOLOR rule below) --
#   useful because a wand of cancellation left in a Bag of Holding is one of
#   the more common ways to accidentally destroy your own bag.
#
# !!! Important note about autopickup !!!
# This file enables autopickup for scrolls by default (see pickup_types
# below). As a result: DO NOT WALK OVER THE GUARANTEED SCROLL OF SCARE
# MONSTER IN SOKOBAN before you've identified it or named it "scare
# monster"/"scare" -- otherwise it will autopickup and go to waste.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# B) Per-Character / Per-Game Settings
# Things you'd change if you were practicing one specific build repeatedly
# (e.g. "always start a Chaotic Human Priest") or personalizing pet names.

# OPTIONS=key:value[,key:value...] is the general form for every setting in
# this file that takes a value; OPTIONS=name (no colon) turns a boolean
# option on, and OPTIONS=!name turns it off.

# Fix your role/race/gender/alignment instead of being prompted every game.
#OPTIONS=name:YourName
#OPTIONS=role:wizard
#OPTIONS=race:human
#OPTIONS=align:neutral
#OPTIONS=gender:random

# Name your starting pets. Uncomment and edit to taste.
#OPTIONS=dogname:Fido
#OPTIONS=catname:Morris
#OPTIONS=horsename:Silver


# The "nameable fruit" replaces "slime mold" throughout the game (in food
# names, the fruit juice potion, etc; default is "slime mold" if unset).
# If you change this, update the MENUCOLOR entry for it in section H so the
# coloring still matches.
OPTIONS=fruit:kiwi
MENUCOLOR=" kiwi"=lightgreen

# Number pad movement. Most players want 0 (move by letters); the digit
# settings are for players who prefer moving with the numeric keypad.
#   0  = move by letters (yuhjklbn)
#   1  = move by numbers; digit '5' acts as a 'G' (go until something
#        interesting) movement prefix
#   2  = like 1, but '5' works as a 'g' prefix instead of 'G'
#   3  = move by numbers using phone keypad layout (1-2-3 above, 7-8-9 below,
#        the reverse of a numpad's usual arrangement)
#   4  = combines 3 with 2, for MS-DOS/old PC Hack compatibility
#  -1  = move by letters, but swap 'y'/'z' for QWERTZ keyboards (e.g. German
#        layout, where those two keys are swapped relative to QWERTY)
OPTIONS=number_pad:0

# Allow saving and loading bones files (deceased characters' levels, with
# their ghost and leftover items, that can turn up in other games).
OPTIONS=!bones

# Allow other local users to send you in-game mail via the mail daemon.
OPTIONS=!mail

# Read the NetHack news file at the start of a game, if the sysadmin has
# provided one.
OPTIONS=news

## Conducts / self-imposed challenges (see the #conduct command in-game).
# These four are the only conducts with an actual option to force them from
# character creation -- everything else #conduct can report (vegetarian,
# vegan, atheist, pacifist, illiterate, weaponless, wishless, genocideless/
# exileless, polypileless, polyselfless, petless, and so on) is tracked
# automatically based on how you actually play; there's no option to force
# those, you just don't do the disqualifying thing (e.g. petless just means
# never taming or ending up with a pet -- no separate setting needed).

# Nudist: start with (and can never gain the benefit of wearing) armor.
#OPTIONS=nudist
# Blind: start the game permanently blind.
#OPTIONS=blind
# Deaf: start the game permanently deaf.
#OPTIONS=deaf
# Pauper: start with no possessions, spells, or skills. Implies nudist too
# (you can still acquire and use items/spells/skills normally once play has
# started -- this only affects your starting inventory).
#OPTIONS=pauper

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# C) Gameplay Settings
# Settings that directly change how you interact with the dungeon.

# Automatically fill your quiver/quiver-sack or ready a suitable weapon when
# you use 'f' (fire) with nothing quivered.
OPTIONS=autoquiver

# Automatically dig while wielding a digging tool, instead of confirming
# each dig. Most players want this OFF -- it's very easy to dig somewhere
# you didn't intend to (e.g. straight down through a trap door you wanted to
# examine first) with it on.
OPTIONS=!autodig

# Automatically open unlocked, closed doors you walk into instead of just
# bumping them.
OPTIONS=autoopen

# Travel/multi-step command speed:
#   walk = redraw the map after every single step (slowest, most visible)
#   run  = redraw less often while running/traveling (faster)
#   teleport = jump straight to the destination with no intermediate redraws
OPTIONS=runmode:run

# Skip the extra confirmation prompt when you search ('s') while a monster
# is adjacent to you. Off because it's usually just noise when you already
# know something's there.
OPTIONS=!safe_wait

# Floor-pile display: when you walk onto a stack of items, this many (or
# more) items shows "there are several/many objects here" instead of always
# popping up the full list. 0 = no limit (always list); 1 = never list
# (always just show the message) since a pile always has at least 1 item;
# default is 5.
# Note: with the aggressive autopickup rules in section D below, most junk
# gets swept up automatically, so leftover piles are usually worth a manual
# look -- that's why this is set low (2) rather than left at the default.
OPTIONS=pile_limit:2

## Paranoid confirmation
# Require a fully spelled-out "yes"/"no" (or an extra confirmation prompt,
# depending on the keyword) for specific situations, instead of accepting a
# bare 'y'/'n' keypress. Useful for avoiding fat-finger disasters. Space-
# separated list; the full set of recognized keywords is:
#   Confirm (alias Paranoia) - require "no" (not just 'n') to reject any
#                               yes/no prompt
#   quit (alias explore)     - "yes" required to quit or switch to
#                               non-scoring explore mode
#   die (alias death)        - "yes" required to die, in explore/debug mode
#   bones                    - "yes" required to save a bones file when
#                               dying in debug mode
#   attack (alias hit)       - "yes" required to attack a peaceful monster
#   wand-break (alias break-wand) - "yes" required to break a wand via apply
#   eat (alias continue)     - "yes" required to keep eating after the first
#                               bite once you're already satiated
#   Were-change               - "yes" required to voluntarily change form
#                               while lycanthropy is under your control
#   pray                      - a plain 'y' is not enough to pray; you must
#                               type the full word
#   trap (alias move-trap)   - a plain 'y' is not enough to walk onto a
#                               known trap, unless it's considered harmless
#   Autoall (alias autoselect-all) - 'y' isn't enough to pick the "select
#                               everything" filter in a Full-style menu
#   swim                      - require the 'm' movement prefix to
#                               deliberately walk into lava or water
#   Remove (alias Takeoff)   - always show a selection menu for the R/T
#                               commands, even when only one item qualifies
OPTIONS=paranoid_confirmation:attack pray quit Remove swim wand-break trap

## Autocomplete
# Auto-complete these #extended commands as you type them, without needing
# to press Tab/Enter to disambiguate. Left disabled by default here because
# it can eat the first keystroke of #chat before you can select which
# monster to talk to.
#AUTOCOMPLETE=zap,pray,force,rub,twoweapon,chat

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D) Auto-Pickup Rules
OPTIONS=autopickup

# Restrict autopickup to just gold ('$') -- the slowest to accumulate junk,
# but safest default if you'd rather pick everything else up by hand.
#OPTIONS=pickup_types:$

# Broader autopickup: gold, gems/rocks, wands, food, scrolls (see the
# warning about scroll of scare monster at the top of this file), potions.
# Spellbooks ('+') are deliberately left out -- they're heavy.
OPTIONS=pickup_types:$?!="/

# Show the weight of each item in <angle brackets> in inventory listings.
OPTIONS=invweight

# Automatically pick back up daggers/arrows/etc. you've thrown.
OPTIONS=pickup_thrown

# Ask for confirmation before an autopickup would push you past this
# encumbrance level. Values, from least to most loaded: Unencumbered,
# Burdened, Stressed, straiNed, overTaxed, overLoaded -- use the capitalized
# letter shown. 'S' means "warn me starting at Stressed".
OPTIONS=pickup_burden:S

## Autopickup exceptions
# AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="PATTERN" where PATTERN is a regular expression
# matched against an item's full inventory description. A leading '<'
# inside the pattern means "always autopickup this, even if pickup_types
# above wouldn't otherwise select it"; a leading '>' means "never
# autopickup this, even if pickup_types would otherwise select it". Order
# matters: the first matching exception wins, top to bottom.

# NEVER autopickup a scroll of scare monster -- see the warning at the top
# of this file for why walking over it unidentified is often a mistake.
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION=">scrolls? (of|called) [Ss]care.*"

# Empty or used-up items (0 charges, drained wands, named "empty"/"junk").
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION=">.*:0).*"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION=">.*wand.*(.*:-1).*"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION=">.* named (empty|junk)"

# Junk: worthless glass gems, plain rocks/flint/statues, and the fake
# Amulet (a real one is caught by the naming trick at the top of this file
# and always autopicked-up regardless).
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="> stone[^<]*$"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="> gray stone[^<]*$"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="> loadstone[^<]*$"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="> flint[^<]*$"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION=">.*cheap plastic imitation of the Amulet of Yendor"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION=">.*worthless piece of.*glass.*"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION=">.*gem.*glass.*"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION=">.*rock.*"
#AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="> rock[^<]*$"
#AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="> glass[^<]*$"

# Always pick up good, reliable food.
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<lichen corpse"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<lizard corpse"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<food ration"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<cram ration"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<tripe ration"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<gunyoki"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<K-ration"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<lembas wafer"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<royal jelly"
#AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<egg"
# Tins are usually worth grabbing (can be tinned later, or eaten as-is).
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="< tin"

## Quest/plot items -- always grab these, they're rare and important.
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION=">.*Amulet of Yendor"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*Amulet of Yendor named ?*"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*Candelabrum of Invocation"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*candelabrum"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*Bell of Opening"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*silver bell"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*Book of the Dead"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*papyrus spellbook"

AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*candle"

# Bullwhips are annoying (they can disarm you), and monsters use them
# aggressively -- worth grabbing so you can deny them to enemies.
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*bullwhip"

## Unidentified armor/rings/amulets
# The unidentified appearance of most armor/rings/amulets is randomized per
# game, so listing them by appearance (rather than by true name, which you
# don't know yet) means you'll pick up the FIRST one of each appearance you
# find (to identify it), while later ones of the same still-unidentified
# appearance get left behind -- avoiding a backpack full of duplicate
# unidentified rings you only needed one of (e.g. rings of invisibility,
# amulets of strangulation, oilskin cloaks). This mostly covers appearances
# that can't be #name'd away from in the usual "call it something" sense;
# it's assumed you'll manually grab or name-exception anything not listed.
# In general, don't end an item-type line with an unanchored '*' -- we want
# to be able to override the category rule for individual named items later
# in the file if needed.

# helmets
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*conical hat"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*crystal helmet"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*plumed helmet"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*etched helmet"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*crested helmet"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*visored helmet"

# robes
# disabled by default; enable if you are a mage
#AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*(white|orange|green|black|purple|blue) robe"

# t-shirts
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION=">.*Hawaiian shirt"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION=">.*T-shirt"

# cloaks
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="< *faded pall"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*slippery cloak"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*apron"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*tattered cape"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*opera cloak"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*ornamental cope"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*dusty cloak"
# Also identified elven cloaks for more polypiling?
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="< *elven cloak"

# shields/bracers
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*polished silver shield"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*smooth shield"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*embossed shield"
AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTION="<.*neon shield"
# "wooden shield" can be a small shield, or shield of drain res or shock res
